        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing: Match Output to the Room, Not the Tank
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common reason a humidifier disappoints is that it is too small for the space. Manufacturers list a square-footage range, and those numbers assume an eight-foot ceiling, a closed door and a reasonably tight room. Open-plan living areas, cathedral ceilings, drafty older houses and very cold outdoor air all push real demand well above the label. As a rule of thumb, buy for the room you have plus a margin: if the room is 300 square feet and opens onto a hallway, shop in the 400-plus range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Output is the number that actually describes capacity. Our top pick is listed at 300 mL per hour on cool mist, which works out to a little over 1.9 gallons a day running flat out; the Honeywell's manual lists 2 gallons a day, and the Vornado lists up to a gallon in 24 hours. Tank size then tells you how often you will refill. A 4.5-liter tank at full cool-mist output lasts around 15 hours, which is why the long runtimes on the box are always quoted at the lowest setting.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a typical bedroom of 150 to 250 square feet, a small ultrasonic or a medium evaporative unit is plenty, and our <a href="/best-humidifier-for-bedroom/">best humidifiers for bedrooms</a> guide narrows that down for quiet overnight use. For living rooms and open areas over 400 square feet, look at units rated well above the space, such as the LV600HH, or see our <a href="/best-humidifier-for-large-room/">best humidifiers for large rooms</a>. If you are trying to humidify more than two rooms, portable units stop making sense, which we cover in the last section.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want the arithmetic rather than a rule of thumb, our guide to <a href="/what-size-humidifier-do-i-need/">what size humidifier you need</a> walks through room volume, air leakage and outdoor temperature, and shows why the same room needs noticeably more moisture in January than in November.
          </p>

